WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
Imagine our mixture already at equilibrium.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
A becomes B while B becomes A at

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.200
the same average rate.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.520
Now add a little more A.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.040
Does B rise at that instant too?

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.080
We added A, not B.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.040
Extra B has to be made by reaction.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.400
Watch those two moments separately: what we change

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.600
directly, then what the reaction does afterwards.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.440
Keep the one-to-one A and B model, with

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.280
temperature and volume fixed.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.240
Concentration means amount per volume.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.000
Briefly add A, then close the container again.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.040
Only A jumps up.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.960
In our model, each direction is faster when

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.200
more of its starting substance is present.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.080
More A makes A to B faster.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:48.800
B has not changed yet, so B to

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.800
A is initially unchanged.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.600
More B is now made than used.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.800
A falls from its jump while B rises.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.680
Their changing amounts bring the rates back towards

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:00.320
equality.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.400
The original-level marks matter: A finishes below its

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.960
immediate jump, but above its original level in

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:07.600
this model.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.560
The reaction uses some of the extra A,

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.040
rather than undoing our addition completely.

00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:16.826
Reset to the original equilibrium for a separate

00:01:16.907 --> 00:01:17.546
experiment.

00:01:18.027 --> 00:01:20.906
Remove some B, then close the container again.

00:01:21.387 --> 00:01:22.906
B drops immediately.

00:01:23.387 --> 00:01:24.666
A does not jump.

00:01:25.227 --> 00:01:27.866
Less B slows B to A at first,

00:01:27.947 --> 00:01:30.266
so A to B is now faster.

00:01:30.587 --> 00:01:32.986
B starts recovering from its drop while A

00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:33.866
decreases.

00:01:34.347 --> 00:01:37.226
That is a shift towards B: the direction

00:01:37.267 --> 00:01:39.426
of the reaction after our change.

00:01:39.707 --> 00:01:42.346
In this model, B still finishes below its

00:01:42.427 --> 00:01:43.306
original level.

00:01:43.787 --> 00:01:46.186
A shift towards B does not mean B

00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:48.346
is higher than it was before we removed

00:01:48.427 --> 00:01:48.746
it.

00:01:49.967 --> 00:01:53.726
Now change temperature instead, keeping matter inside and

00:01:53.807 --> 00:01:54.766
volume fixed.

00:01:55.247 --> 00:01:58.446
Two nitrogen dioxide molecules join to form one

00:01:58.607 --> 00:02:00.686
dinitrogen tetroxide molecule.

00:02:01.247 --> 00:02:05.486
Joining releases heat: we call that direction exothermic,

00:02:05.647 --> 00:02:06.926
meaning heat-releasing.

00:02:07.487 --> 00:02:09.246
Splitting absorbs heat.

00:02:09.487 --> 00:02:12.206
Heating favours the heat-absorbing direction in the new

00:02:12.287 --> 00:02:15.646
equilibrium, so there is more nitrogen dioxide and

00:02:15.727 --> 00:02:17.726
less dinitrogen tetroxide.

00:02:18.287 --> 00:02:23.006
Nitrogen dioxide is brown; dinitrogen tetroxide is colourless.

00:02:23.487 --> 00:02:25.926
The warmer mixture is therefore browner.

00:02:26.207 --> 00:02:29.086
Cooling favours the heat-releasing direction and gives the

00:02:29.247 --> 00:02:30.206
opposite result.

00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:33.766
First identify which direction releases heat.

00:02:34.047 --> 00:02:36.166
Heating does not always give more of the

00:02:36.207 --> 00:02:37.166
written product.

00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:41.566
Watch the concentration graph during an idealised rapid

00:02:41.647 --> 00:02:42.806
temperature change.

00:02:43.087 --> 00:02:46.046
We keep volume fixed and transfer no reacting

00:02:46.327 --> 00:02:48.606
material into or out of the container.

00:02:49.007 --> 00:02:51.966
So neither concentration jumps just because we heated

00:02:52.047 --> 00:02:52.206
it.

00:02:52.687 --> 00:02:56.046
The reaction changes the concentrations afterwards.

00:02:56.367 --> 00:02:59.966
Heating changes how readily the opposing reactions happen,

00:03:00.047 --> 00:03:03.246
and for this heat-releasing joining reaction their previous

00:03:03.327 --> 00:03:05.886
balance is lost in favour of splitting.

00:03:06.527 --> 00:03:10.926
Nitrogen dioxide rises while dinitrogen tetroxide falls.

00:03:11.567 --> 00:03:13.726
Hold the new temperature steady as the mixture

00:03:13.807 --> 00:03:15.406
approaches a new balance.

00:03:15.887 --> 00:03:18.286
The missing vertical jump is useful here because

00:03:18.367 --> 00:03:21.246
our stated change was temperature alone at fixed

00:03:21.327 --> 00:03:21.806
volume.

00:03:22.287 --> 00:03:24.366
It would not identify the cause of every

00:03:24.447 --> 00:03:25.406
unknown graph.

00:03:26.634 --> 00:03:29.033
Try a new change to our one-to-one model.

00:03:29.514 --> 00:03:33.113
Start at equilibrium, keep temperature and volume fixed,

00:03:33.194 --> 00:03:36.033
add B, then close the container again.

00:03:36.394 --> 00:03:38.793
Each rate increases when more of its starting

00:03:38.874 --> 00:03:40.273
substance is present.

00:03:40.554 --> 00:03:42.953
Which concentration changes immediately?

00:03:43.274 --> 00:03:45.833
Which direction is then faster, and how do

00:03:45.994 --> 00:03:47.673
A and B change afterwards?

00:03:48.234 --> 00:03:50.233
Pause to work through both moments.

00:04:05.634 --> 00:04:09.033
Only B jumps because only B was added.

00:04:09.314 --> 00:04:11.953
More B makes B to A faster, while

00:04:12.034 --> 00:04:14.273
A to B is initially unchanged.

00:04:14.914 --> 00:04:17.153
A is made faster than it is used,

00:04:17.234 --> 00:04:18.673
so A increases.

00:04:19.314 --> 00:04:21.073
B falls from its jump as it is

00:04:21.154 --> 00:04:22.193
converted to A.

00:04:22.754 --> 00:04:25.473
The changing concentrations bring the rates back towards

00:04:25.554 --> 00:04:26.273
balance.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.580
Le Chatelier’s principle summarises the response: a disturbed

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:34.620
equilibrium reacts in a direction that opposes the

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.180
imposed change.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:39.860
Our rate comparison explains how that response happens.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:43.740
A useful anchor is direct change first, reaction

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:44.380
follows.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:48.300
For temperature, identify heat release before predicting the

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:48.940
direction.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:51.820
Treat writing heat beside the equation as a

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.700
memory aid, not as a literal concentration of

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:55.460
heat.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.900
Next, we will change the volume of a

00:04:57.980 --> 00:04:59.460
gaseous equilibrium.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:00.940
Here are the useful notes.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.180
Pause to copy if you need longer.
